<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 3 – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Trip to the Beach</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Pack for a Trip.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Describe quantities and dates using numbers up to a hundred. interactive: Sustain conversations on a variety of topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe quantities needed for a trip using numbers (e.g., 'I need three towels and two hats.'). Can initiate and sustain conversations about beach trips and respond to questions (e.g., 'Where are you going this weekend?' 'We are going to the beach.' 'Do you like the beach?' 'Yes, it's fun.'). Can answer, in collaboration with peers, questions and discuss plans for beach trips (e.g., 'What do you bring to the beach? I bring sunscreen.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; background-color: #f0f0f0;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; background-color: #f0f0f0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; background-color: #f0f0f0;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or realia of beach items (e.g., towel, swimsuit, sunscreen, sun hat, beach ball, cooler). The teacher asks students, "What do you see?" and encourages choral and individual responses. Then, the teacher asks, "Do you like the beach?" and "What do you do at the beach?" to activate prior knowledge related to the theme "Let's Pack for a Trip." and scenario "A Trip to the Beach." Students quickly share ideas in pairs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a swimsuit and says, "I am going to the beach. I need a swimsuit." (modelling 'going to' and quantities implicitly). The teacher then picks up three imaginary towels and says, "I need three towels. Please, bring your swimsuit." (modelling imperative). The teacher clearly articulates the sounds /b/ in 'beach' and 'bucket' and /p/ in 'pack' and 'picnic', asking students to repeat. The teacher demonstrates simple conversational turns: "Where are you going?" "I am going to the beach." "What do you bring?" "I bring sunscreen."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using questions and examples:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'Please pack your flip-flops,' what should you do?" (Expected answer: 'Put flip-flops in your bag.')</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'I am going to the beach' about the past or the future?" (Expected answer: 'Future.')</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two friends, Sara and Leo, planning a beach trip. The dialogue is presented on a large chart or projected, with accompanying simple illustrations of items. The genre is an informal conversation, register is friendly, and format is a dialogue script.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br>
                    Sara: "Hi Leo! Where are you going this weekend?"<br>
                    Leo: "Hi Sara! We are going to the beach! I like to swim there."<br>
                    Sara: "Oh, fun! What do you bring to the beach?"<br>
                    Leo: "I always bring one towel, sunscreen, and two floaties. My mom says, 'Please pack your sun hat!'"<br>
                    Sara: "Wow! I sometimes go to the beach. I need to pack my beach ball. Do you like to collect shells?"<br>
                    Leo: "Yes! I collect many shells every time. We are going to build a big sandcastle!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sustain conversations on a variety of topics, specifically about beach trips and packing. Describe quantities needed for a trip using numbers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the teacher read the dialogue (or listen to an audio recording) and then answer oral gist questions in pairs, reporting back to the class:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where are Sara and Leo going?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does Leo always bring?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does Leo like to build?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using Present Simple (affirmative and question forms), Imperatives (polite instructions), and 'Going to' for future plans. They will use vocabulary related to beach items and activities (beach, towel, sunscreen, sun hat, swimsuit, pack, bring, swim, build, collect, fun, sunny, sandy, cooler, beach ball, flip-flops, floaties) and numbers to describe quantities. Pronunciation focus on /b/ and /p/ sounds, and phonemic awareness by spelling and blending words like 'beach' and 'pack'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Packing List Chain"</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher starts by saying, "I am going to the beach. I am going to pack one swimsuit." The first student repeats the sentence and adds one new item with a quantity: "I am going to the beach. I am going to pack one swimsuit and two towels." The next student repeats the previous sentence and adds another item, focusing on numbers up to ten and correct pronunciation of /b/ and /p/ words. The teacher corrects pronunciation and quantity descriptions as needed.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: "Ask and Answer for the Trip"</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In pairs, students take turns asking and answering questions using visual prompts (e.g., pictures of different beach items with varying quantities).
                            Example questions:</p>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you bring to the beach?" (Student A) -> "I bring three towels and one sun hat." (Student B)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you like to swim?" (Student A) -> "Yes, I like to swim!" (Student B)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are you going to do?" (Student A) -> "I am going to build a sandcastle." (Student B)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students also practice polite imperatives: "Please pack sunscreen." "Please bring your beach ball."</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check: "Plan a Picnic"</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. Each pair is given a simple picture of a picnic basket and a list of items with numbers (e.g., 5 sandwiches, 2 bottles of juice, 1 blanket). They take turns discussing their picnic plans for the beach, describing quantities and using 'going to'.
                            Example conversation:</p>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We are going to have a picnic at the beach. We need five sandwiches."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"And we need two bottles of juice. Please, bring the cooler."</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">They check each other's use of vocabulary and sentence structure, gently correcting if needed.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Our Beach Trip Plan" Role-play</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of three. Each group imagines they are planning a trip to the beach together. They need to discuss what they are going to bring, what they like to do, and who is responsible for packing certain items. They must use numbers up to twenty to describe quantities and practice polite instructions (imperatives).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups receive a scenario card (e.g., "You are going to the beach for a day. Discuss what to pack and what activities you will do.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students collaboratively decide on 5-7 items they need to pack, specifying quantities (e.g., "We need three beach balls and four towels.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They discuss activities they will do using "going to" and present simple ("I like to swim," "We are going to build a sandcastle").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They assign roles for packing or bringing items using polite imperatives (e.g., "Please, bring two sun hats, Maria." "Don't forget the cooler, Juan.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group practices their conversation. They then perform their "Our Beach Trip Plan" role-play for another group or the whole class.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, communicative task completion. Students should initiate and sustain conversations, describe quantities using numbers, and use polite requests.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Our Beach Trip Plan" role-play from Stage 4. The teacher observes individual and group performance, making notes on a simple checklist. Peer groups also provide feedback using a simplified rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /b/ and /p/ sounds).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (beach items, verbs like 'pack,' 'bring,' 'swim').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of communication.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and interaction with peers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of 'going to' for plans.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of imperatives for polite instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to describe quantities using numbers up to twenty.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick written or oral exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I can say about a beach trip is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I spoke English about..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I still need to practice is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Self-reflection and teacher insight)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of talking about your beach trip was easy for you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging, and how can you improve your speaking next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? The role-play activity (Stage 4) was very engaging and allowed students to use the language freely in a meaningful context. The focus on polite imperatives encouraged good classroom communication.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson? Some students struggled with spontaneously forming questions. More explicit practice with question words like "what," "where," and "when" in simple Q&A formats could be beneficial. I will also incorporate more visual prompts for numbers up to a hundred to solidify quantity descriptions for stronger students.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will use the vocabulary and grammar practiced today to write a short paragraph about their ideal beach trip or what they would pack, transitioning from spoken plans to written expression.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>